<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    angr test_decompiling_optimized_memcpy (memcpy/memmove @ 0x42cca0, an MSVC
    CRT optimized Duff's-device copy): seven image-base-relative jump tables
    drive the small-count tail dispatch.  angr recovers all 7 switches; kuna
    recovers ONE and degrades the other SIX indirect jumps to
    "Treating indirect jump as call" (an invalid / misleading computed call).

    Root cause (per docs/features/optimized-memcpy-6301a9, REVISED): the six
    failing tables do NOT fail in JumpBasic guard analysis.  They fail earlier,
    in the per-table partial-flow rebuild.  kuna recovers one table first
    (switch over a 4-entry table whose case-0 body sits at 0x42ce24); then every
    OTHER indirect jump spins up its own fresh partial clone that re-clones the
    now-recovered table into the partial's jumpvec, and the partial's
    `collect_edges` iterates that table's case targets and calls `target(addr)`.
    The case body at 0x42ce24 (reachable only as that table's case-0 target) was
    never decoded into the partial's `visited` snapshot (it is decoded into the
    PARENT flow only after the whole recover_jump_tables pass returns), so
    `target(0x42ce24)` throws "Could not find op at target address", the partial
    pipeline errors, the table fails FailNormal, and flow.rs truncates the
    indirect jump to a call.

    Upstream Ghidra avoids this: it builds ONE shared partial per
    recoverJumpTables call and runs generateBlocks/collectEdges only once, while
    the sibling tables are still EMPTY (addresstable filled later, in
    recoverAddresses), so collectEdges iterates 0 case targets and never calls
    target() on an undecoded body.

    Fix surface = a default-off ArchOption (unrolledguard): inside a
    jump-table-recovery partial clone, when collect_edges cannot resolve a
    recovered sibling table's case-target op (the address was not decoded into
    this partial), SKIP that edge instead of propagating the error: exactly
    reproducing upstream's partial CFG (the partial BRANCHIND has no
    case-target out-edges for the undecoded targets, the same "assume no
    branches out" shape the findJumpTable==0 path already uses).

    Pass 1 (default, option off): assertion #1 proves the bug reproduces. Six
    indirect jumps render as computed (code *)() calls.
    Pass 2 (option unrolledguard on): assertions #2/#3 prove the partial-flow
    skip recovers the tables. The computed calls are gone and switch
    dispatches appear.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x42cca0">

</bytechunk>
<!-- Stub the lone external tail-call target (jmp 0x439674 at 0x42cce2) with a
     bare ret so the bytechunk is self-contained. -->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x439674">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x42cca0" name="memcpy_opt"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>lo fu memcpy_opt</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option unrolledguard on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<!-- The two passes' `print C` outputs are concatenated into one stream the
     stringmatches scan.  Pass 1 (option off): the seven jump tables yield exactly
     ONE recovered switch and SEVEN computed `**(code **)()` calls (the six
     interleaved tables that fail the partial-flow rebuild, plus one more).
     Pass 2 (option unrolledguard on): every table recovers, so the computed
     calls drop to ZERO and the switch count rises to 16.  We assert on the
     `code **` computed-call rendering (the load-bearing CFG effect) and the
     `switch(` count; the stale "Treating indirect jump as call" WARNING comment
     is left behind by the re-decompile's warning store and is NOT a reliable
     post-fix signal (the same reason the switchmultipred test asserts on the
     call rendering, not the warning text). -->
<stringmatch name="unrolledguard #1: option off, the interleaved dispatches degrade to computed calls (bug reproduced); option on adds ZERO new ones (7 pass1 + 0 pass2)" min="7" max="7">code \*\*</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="unrolledguard #2: option on recovers every interleaved table as a switch (1 off + 16 on = 17)" min="17" max="17">switch\(</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
